WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.540
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:05.040
SARAH HANSEN: Today,
on Chalk Radio,

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:08.400
making calculus a
little less abstract.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:09.900
GIGLIOLA STAFFILANI:
We put together

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:13.200
a lot of problems that
come from concepts that we

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.660
see in real life.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:17.010
If it's connected with
something which is more real,

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:21.390
you are more willing
to work through that.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.820
SARAH HANSEN: I'm your
host, Sarah Hansen.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:27.000
Calculus has an
intimidating reputation,

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.430
but our guest today
has been experimenting

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:33.450
with creative new ways to
teach this complex subject.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:36.880
Gigliola Staffilani is a
Professor of Mathematics,

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:39.720
and one of the lead instructors
of introductory course,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:43.650
Calculus I. And she's found
that with a little help,

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:47.040
students often find calculus
to be more intuitive than they

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:48.917
first expect.

00:00:48.917 --> 00:00:51.000
GIGLIOLA STAFFILANI: I
feel like calculus actually

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.320
gets introduced without students
really knowing about it--

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.420
like when they
start doing algebra,

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:01.320
and they start talking
about functions, and also

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.410
by watching movies,
documentaries, and so on.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:07.470
You often learn about
function velocity

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.840
and you learn that
there is a direction,

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.550
there is a magnitude,
which is the speed.

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:15.550
And it's kind of like
going to a foreign country.

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:17.910
And if you just live
there for a little bit,

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:20.970
after a couple of weeks,
you pick up a few words.

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.910
But then, it's only when
you actually take a class

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:26.878
that you put all this together.

00:01:26.878 --> 00:01:28.420
SARAH HANSEN: But
sometimes, students

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:32.950
get stuck in translation when
moving across disciplines.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:35.410
GIGLIOLA STAFFILANI: I
think us mathematicians

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:39.040
like to use way less common
language like, for example,

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:42.400
most common notation
for a variable is x.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.540
Sometimes, we ask y, the
function is always an f.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:48.680
We just are very
familiar in using that,

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:50.950
and it's very common
among mathematicians.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:54.190
But I realized, for
example, that it's not

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:57.550
a common notation for
the same kind of question

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:01.960
when you read books that
come from chemistry.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:05.200
In math, you want
to reduce everything

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.780
to a mathematical
concept and you

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:10.600
don't want to be distracted
with names and maybe

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.340
cumbersome type of notation.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:14.570
You just don't want
that to distract you.

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:16.905
But in chemistry, the
name of the variable

00:02:16.905 --> 00:02:18.280
is important
because you probably

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:21.850
need to remember what that
means within a certain reaction

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:23.140
or something like that.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:25.540
It is very hard, I
think, for a student

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:30.160
to, when he or she starts
taking a different class

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:35.260
after calculus, to realize that
actually, they know already

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:37.090
what they are supposed to know.

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:40.330
They just think that they
don't because the notation

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:43.518
is completely different.

00:02:43.518 --> 00:02:45.310
SARAH HANSEN: To help
students get grounded

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:48.760
in the world of calculus,
Gigliola and her colleagues

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:52.840
have brought visual learning
methods into the classroom.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:56.590
GIGLIOLA STAFFILANI: When we
put together the calculus class

00:02:56.590 --> 00:03:00.820
that we have on MITx,
it was very important

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:04.540
for us to give a
tool to the students

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:07.000
to actually draw pictures.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:11.860
In calculus, it's very important
to understand the relationship

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:14.450
between the formula--

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:18.740
so the analysis, what we
call mathematical analysis,

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:21.190
so there is a formula
written with variables--

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:22.980
and what that
formula represents.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:27.320
So for example, we can write
a line with an equation,

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:28.900
but we also can draw a line.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:32.680
And we need to understand why
the two objects are exactly

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:35.920
the same, and if we
change a parameter, how

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.527
that line or other
curve changes.

00:03:40.527 --> 00:03:42.110
SARAH HANSEN:
Sometimes, those methods

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:45.122
might look like a
demonstration in class.

00:03:45.122 --> 00:03:47.330
GIGLIOLA STAFFILANI: One
that I have used in my class

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:50.840
at some point, which is
really very hard to grasp,

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:53.360
is Lagrange multipliers.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:58.880
This is a method that we
use to find maximum minimum

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:00.470
with constraints.

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:02.700
Let's think about
sphere, for example,

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:05.450
when you have a
surface that grows,

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:08.960
so the maximum radius
until the sphere touches

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:10.700
the boundary of a square.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:15.148
When that touching happens,
then the two orthogonal vectors

00:04:15.148 --> 00:04:16.190
are going to be parallel.

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:19.459
That's really what
the system tells you.

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:22.400
SARAH HANSEN: If, like me,
you're not a mathematician,

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:26.210
you might be a little lost
in all these technical terms.

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:28.790
But just wait until
Gigliola describes

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:33.758
how she turns these abstract
ideas into a physical example.

00:04:33.758 --> 00:04:35.800
GIGLIOLA STAFFILANI: So
I usually bring a balloon

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:38.500
that I start inflating in a box.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:40.330
And then, when it
touches the box,

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:42.310
it's clear that
the side of the box

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:45.472
is totally tangent to
the side of my surface.

00:04:45.472 --> 00:04:46.930
And that's where
the normal vectors

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:48.138
are orthogonal to each other.

00:04:48.138 --> 00:04:52.960
So this is intuitively
visible, and it tells you

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:55.130
that, really, these
vectors are parallel.

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:57.100
And then, setting up
the system becomes

00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:59.620
more like writing
down what it means

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:01.600
to have these two
vectors in a parallel way

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:03.770
instead of just
remembering the algorithm,

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:06.760
having to take all these
derivatives that equal to zero

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:07.880
and things like that.

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:11.170
So it is very
important, whenever

00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:14.380
you have the opportunity
to visualize an object,

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:18.340
it is important to
do that except that,

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:22.240
as you become more and more
sophisticated in mathematics,

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:24.410
that becomes harder and harder.

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:28.932
But whenever you can,
anything you can use is good.

00:05:28.932 --> 00:05:30.640
SARAH HANSEN: Gigliola
and her colleagues

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:32.680
have incorporated
digital applets

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:35.260
to bring these kinds of
visual representations

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:37.660
into learning experiences.

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:40.030
Applets are also
expanding the kinds

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:43.600
of problems they're able
to challenge students with.

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:45.250
GIGLIOLA STAFFILANI:
So some problems

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:50.290
are like a yes and no question
or like a plug-in a number.

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:53.260
And some other problems,
in particular in calculus,

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:57.550
have some kind of a complicated
formula or a drawing

00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:03.070
or something which is not
easy to grade or to decide

00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:05.150
whether it's correct or not.

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:08.740
So the sketching tool
was very important

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:13.480
because there are problems in
calculus in which you don't

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:17.410
need to be extremely
precise, but with a drawing,

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:20.820
you can show us whether you
understand the concept or not.

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:22.480
But then, of course,
my drawing might

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:25.360
be different than your drawing
although they're both correct,

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:27.580
they both reflects
what we ask you to do.

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:31.270
And this was a complicated
type of problem

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:34.220
and a very difficult
problem to grade.

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:36.850
So the sketching
tool, I think, gave us

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:40.970
a way of grading questions and
the possibility to the student

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:45.280
to solve questions in a way
which was exactly what we want

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:49.480
them to do instead of a
complicated, cumbersome way

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:53.187
for us to decide whether
you understand or not.

00:06:53.187 --> 00:06:55.520
SARAH HANSEN: As she experiments
with new teaching tools

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:58.340
for Calculus I,
Gigliola has also

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:02.810
grappled with inequities in
the field of mathematics.

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:07.100
GIGLIOLA STAFFILANI: We thought
about also giving the chance

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:09.890
to students to solve the problem
kind of like a little video

00:07:09.890 --> 00:07:11.870
games or things like that.

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:15.080
But it's just really
complicated to do that.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:22.460
We also thought that will have
been not a fair way of dealing

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:23.750
with this because--

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:26.150
I think there are
studies on that--

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:29.450
that the boys tend to do video
games or work on video games

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:30.650
much more than girls.

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:33.410
So if we set up
something like that,

00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:36.200
then we were afraid
that the girls will not

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:38.280
be interested in it.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:40.620
And so we decided not
to even go that way.

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:44.360
So to make it fair and make it
interesting for all, not just

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:47.340
for a category of people.

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:48.840
SARAH HANSEN: And
as a mother, she's

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:51.810
witnessed firsthand how
biases are formed years

00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:55.740
before students even
reach the college level.

00:07:55.740 --> 00:07:58.290
GIGLIOLA STAFFILANI: I have
twins, a boy and a girl.

00:07:58.290 --> 00:07:59.550
And now, they are 16.

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:03.510
And I witnessed from the time
they were in middle school

00:08:03.510 --> 00:08:06.720
how different they were
treated because one was a boy,

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:07.980
one was a girl.

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:10.490
And the reaction that
they have to the way

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:11.490
they are treated, right?

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:15.810
So my son, for
example, is kind of--

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:17.550
I wouldn't say he
shows off, but he

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:21.390
likes to be thought as somebody
that picks hard classes.

00:08:21.390 --> 00:08:23.850
And my daughter,
who is just as good

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:26.020
as he is and she
likes her class,

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:27.480
but she hides it completely.

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:30.330
She doesn't say that
she's taking hard classes

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:32.400
because that's not
considered to be

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:36.299
attractive for a young
woman or older woman.

00:08:36.299 --> 00:08:38.159
Why for boys it's OK?

00:08:38.159 --> 00:08:40.770
So there is a lot
of social pressure

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:46.380
and also a lot of different
reactions to the behavior

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:48.120
from the teachers.

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:52.020
So it's very acceptable
if a girl is not really

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:55.830
putting too much effort
in science and math,

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:58.470
and it's less
acceptable for boys.

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:01.440
So yeah, unfortunately,
from that early on,

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:05.200
women, young women and young
men, are treated differently.

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:08.880
And I think it really
takes a lot of effort

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:13.830
for the young women who want to
do more science and more math

00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:17.370
because I think they just
have to go against very

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:19.450
high social pressure.

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:22.780
So then, by the time
I see them, undergrad

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:28.710
at MIT, we know it's
about 49% women, 51% men.

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:31.650
In the math department,
I think it's around 30%

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:33.630
women in math major.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:35.550
And then, when you
go to grad school,

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:38.790
there are maybe 18%, 20% women.

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:43.640
And when you go to faculty,
well, there are four of us.

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:45.530
SARAH HANSEN: Is
there one small thing

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:49.333
that educators could do to try
to make this situation better?

00:09:49.333 --> 00:09:50.750
GIGLIOLA STAFFILANI:
Yeah, I think

00:09:50.750 --> 00:09:55.730
being aware that they themselves
have implicit biases would

00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:57.530
be already a big step.

00:09:57.530 --> 00:10:00.000
And I do have implicit
biases myself.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.340
I know that, but
I'm aware of it.

00:10:03.340 --> 00:10:06.020
So I'm trying to go in
the opposite direction I

00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:09.660
will actually be tempted to
go without thinking about it.

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:12.140
So I think being
aware of the issue,

00:10:12.140 --> 00:10:16.650
being aware that when you ask
a question in your math class

00:10:16.650 --> 00:10:20.570
and you might be a woman
teacher, you look at the boys

00:10:20.570 --> 00:10:22.370
more than the girls,
which is something

00:10:22.370 --> 00:10:23.810
that happens very often.

00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:26.940
Being aware of that,
that's already a big step.

00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:31.280
So next time you
try to do 50-50.

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:35.360
If all the problems are about
baseball players, and soccer,

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:35.900
and--

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:37.350
well, now, the girls
do soccer and they

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:38.392
are even better than men.

00:10:38.392 --> 00:10:39.860
So that's great.

00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:43.550
But there were problems that
were written 50 years ago

00:10:43.550 --> 00:10:47.720
and they were all for boys
because those are the people

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:49.040
that would take those classes.

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:52.730
So being aware of this
and what difference

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:57.630
could make if you just phrase
the problem in a different way,

00:10:57.630 --> 00:11:00.800
I think it's very important.

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:02.300
SARAH HANSEN: At
the end of the day,

00:11:02.300 --> 00:11:03.860
Gigliola hopes
that students will

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:05.720
feel invested in the
questions they're

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:07.270
answering in Calculus I.

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:08.770
GIGLIOLA STAFFILANI:
We put together

00:11:08.770 --> 00:11:12.100
a lot of problems that
come from concepts that we

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:13.510
see in real life.

00:11:13.510 --> 00:11:17.920
That really helps, I think, also
in accepting the difficulties

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:20.380
that you face when you actually
have to solve a problem.

00:11:20.380 --> 00:11:22.780
If it's connected with
something which is more real,

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:26.150
you are more willing
to work through that.

00:11:26.150 --> 00:11:28.730
So for example, one
problem that I like,

00:11:28.730 --> 00:11:32.380
which actually has to do with
solving some linear equations,

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:35.980
is a question on
how many bagels I'm

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:40.660
supposed to buy versus how
many croissants and how many--

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:43.900
if the classes want a certain--

00:11:43.900 --> 00:11:47.260
or likes more sweets or
salty or things like that.

00:11:47.260 --> 00:11:50.200
So it's a simple
problem, but then

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:52.900
when you have to translate
into math and find a solution,

00:11:52.900 --> 00:11:55.810
you have to use relatively
sophisticated tools

00:11:55.810 --> 00:11:58.295
to do that if there's
more than one variable.

00:11:58.295 --> 00:12:00.170
SARAH HANSEN: With an
important outcome, too.

00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:00.680
GIGLIOLA STAFFILANI: Yeah.

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:01.660
Right.

00:12:01.660 --> 00:12:03.700
I usually bring that
stuff in this class

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:06.970
afterwards-- croissants,
and bagels, and donuts.

00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:09.970
And then, I try to say,
how would you attack this?

00:12:09.970 --> 00:12:11.240
How would you do this?

00:12:11.240 --> 00:12:13.850
So maybe some questions
from biology or questions

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:18.980
from chemistry or something that
has at least a first step which

00:12:18.980 --> 00:12:23.170
one can understand using
calculus to give you the idea

00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:25.510
that this is not like
some abstract nonsense

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:28.000
that we forced you
to take at MIT,

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:31.630
but it's something that's
very important to, like I said

00:12:31.630 --> 00:12:33.520
from the very beginning,
it's a language

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:35.320
that you need to
acquire in order

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:39.310
to attack any basic
question that might come up

00:12:39.310 --> 00:12:42.400
in science in general which
has not already been answered.

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:44.125
Which is something
that they would

00:12:44.125 --> 00:12:47.768
love to be in the position
of doing in your life, right?

00:12:47.768 --> 00:12:48.685
Answer a new question.

00:12:50.913 --> 00:12:52.830
SARAH HANSEN: If you're
interested in teaching

00:12:52.830 --> 00:12:55.230
with Gigliola's
calculus materials,

00:12:55.230 --> 00:12:58.560
head over to MIT's Open
Learning library site

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:02.070
where you'll find her single
variable calculus MOOCs.

00:13:02.070 --> 00:13:04.130
Thank you so much for listening.

